{
  "setup": "Physical C5 sheet; two native force ticks g(x)=x from (0,R,0). The four orthogonal old position/coin modes retain their labels. A separate initialized R probe coin then takes five ordinary +/-1, signed H, sheet steps. A detector couples only to the named physical cell and selected arriving rail(s); all other coordinates are unread. Counterfactual counting propagates history multiplicities and divides by 2 per H. The signed law squares the summed real amplitudes separately in each complete cell. Both use the same routing and detector.",
  "readout": "A terminal controlled-X flips a carried zero bit at the chosen site and rail(s). Afterwards every walker can continue through a common streaming-only step; only the bit is read. The port-conditioned permutation is local on each sheet fibre. If old bases are separate physical places, the marker coupling must be present there or the modes coherently brought to one device. Marker placement, gating/clock, clean bit and readout of that bit are supplied.",
  "row_columns": [
    "probe_ticks",
    "denominator",
    "terminal_no",
    "terminal_yes",
    "parity_even",
    "parity_odd"
  ],
  "instruments": "Always-on XOR measures visit parity without intermediate read. The sink swaps a hit on each tick into distinct initially blank outgoing modes carrying time and rails. A finite stopped sink and a reversible single-bit OR are different: overwriting two inputs by one output loses reversibility, so a destructive sink pays a reservoir/reset cost. All first bins and no-capture probability are retained. Numerators use the denominators for their actual number of H layers, including the initial two force layers.",
